{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/357299-shan-iatu-unity-or-lack-of","id":357299,"title":"Shan'iatu unity or lack of","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-01-02T00:18:00-08:00","id":357299,"title":"Shan'iatu unity or lack of","content":"Were the Shan'iatu a unified political entity? Some of the entries in Deceived seem to suggest they were very capable of disagreement or even open conflict.<br /> <br /> Is it possible that the Shan'iatu had distinct factions? Wars? Did a Shan'iatu ever murder another Shan'iatu (and were they even capable of being ended that way)?<br /> <br /> How would such nonconformists feel about the Rite of Return? Would they have had to be brought to heel, either subjugated or destroyed, or could some Shan'iatu have survived the Rite undertaken by unfriendly comrades? Might some Shan'iatu still survive in heavily diminished, more humble form somewhere? <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T02:26:06-08:00","id":357353,"title":"","content":"I think the Book of the Deceived makes it quite, quite clear that the Shan'iatu were most certainly not a unified political entity. It makes it almost equally clear that the Shan'iatu never attempted to outright kill one another (barring the final moments of the Rite), as I think the death of one of their own would have been something the writer in Deceived's opening would have remarked upon. Note the reluctance they display when it comes to the thought of acting violently against their brothers and sisters. It also makes it sound like the Shan'iatu were unified when it came to the Rite, barring the plot of the Akhem-Urtu Guildmasters to undermine the others and the way they were in turn betrayed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-07T08:24:49-08:00","id":362470,"title":"","content":"I found it very odd that the 8 Deceived Shan'iatu were all male. I'd be interested in knowing if the Shan'iatu were originally an even split of 21 male/21 female, and if so if another Guild had an overwhelmingly female Guild leadership presence. <br /> <br /> It certainly sounds like the Shan'iatu of the 5 Arisen Guilds certainly conspired in a way to exclude the Akhem-Utru from the details of the Rite. Perhaps they were jealous of Nomenclature, and almost certainly they suspected betrayal thus leading them to their own betrayal of the Deceived temakhs. I wouldn't be surprised if the Akhem-Urtu being seen as First Among Equals for centuries led them to be a block unto themselves that the leaders of the other Guilds didn't like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-07T09:43:32-08:00","id":362556,"title":"","content":"I thought the first chapter of <b>Book of the Deceived</b> said something about the temakh and gender, but I could be mistaken.<br /> <br /> They're alien god-spirits, at any rate, and presumably don't care about their manifestation any more than a Mummy identifies with gender. So it's fairly mutable I imagine. With that being said, they were servants created to help and guide humanity (agents of the Principle?) so their similarities with their wards might be more than, say, one of the strix. Not that 5,000 years of vengeful psychosis doesn't wear on your god-spirit psyche, but hey","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-07T14:09:55-08:00","id":362864,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/357299-shan-iatu-unity-or-lack-of?p=362556#post362556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought the first chapter of <b>Book of the Deceived</b> said something about the temakh and gender, but I could be mistaken.<br /> <br /> They're alien god-spirits, at any rate, and presumably don't care about their manifestation any more than a Mummy identifies with gender. So it's fairly mutable I imagine. With that being said, they were servants created to help and guide humanity (agents of the Principle?) so their similarities with their wards might be more than, say, one of the strix. Not that 5,000 years of vengeful psychosis doesn't wear on your god-spirit psyche, but hey</div> </div> </div> </div>All seven temakh are male, and Book of the Deceived makes sure to discuss that in the context of gender dysphoria. <br /> <br /> That said, the same book also describes the &quot;men and women&quot; of the Shan'iatu, so we know some of the others were female.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}